spoz2 0.1

# dosh-do described in SPOZ2: intended behaviour, not implementation.
# Distilled starting at version 9.0.0.  History starts here —
# earlier versions are not reconstructed.
#
# SPOZ2 conventions: an invariant is a named rule bound to versions
# (since, and until when retired).  History is append-only: a change
# of intent is a NEW entry with the same name and a later "since";
# old entries stay and remain authoritative for their versions.
# Never edit or delete an existing entry.  "spoz2 guard" enforces
# this; "spoz2 add" and "spoz2 retire" are the safe write paths.

system dosh-do
  dosh (do shell) turns a request written in natural language into a
  suggested shell command.  The user types what they want as plain
  words after "dosh"; dosh asks an LLM for a command that fulfils the
  request and presents it for the user to accept or decline.  It is an
  assistant at the shell prompt, not an autonomous executor: the human
  stays in charge of what actually runs.

invariant no-execution-without-confirmation
  since 9.0.0
  A suggested command is never executed without the user's explicit
  confirmation.  The suggestion step and the execution step are always
  separate, because the LLM's output cannot be trusted to run
  unreviewed on the user's machine.  This is the core safety promise
  of the tool; breaking it silently would turn an advisor into an
  agent with the user's shell privileges.

invariant suggestion-carries-explanation
  since 9.0.0
  Every suggested command is accompanied by a plain-language
  explanation of what the command does, so the user can give informed
  confirmation rather than blindly trusting a one-liner they may not
  be able to read.

invariant destructive-suggestions-are-flagged
  since 9.0.0
  When a suggested command would make changes to the system (modify,
  move or delete files, alter state) the suggestion includes a warning
  saying so.  Read-only queries and state-changing commands must not
  look the same at the confirmation prompt.

invariant natural-language-is-the-interface
  since 9.0.0
  The user expresses their request as ordinary natural language given
  directly as the arguments to dosh, with no query syntax or quoting
  ritual to learn.  A handful of reserved words (config, help) are
  subcommands; everything else is treated as the request itself.

invariant suggestions-fit-the-host-system
  since 9.0.0
  Suggested commands are tailored to the operating system dosh is
  running on, so that a suggestion accepted by the user actually works
  there rather than being generic advice for some other platform.

invariant llm-is-user-configurable
  since 9.0.0
  Which LLM is used, and how, is the user's choice: dosh exposes its
  LLM settings through "dosh config" rather than hard-wiring a
  provider.  dosh's value is the workflow around the model, not any
  one model.
